{"pair_id": "pair_low_7b261d32c799", "family_id": "tantra_hist_pd", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for tantra_hist_pd (continue low family boost)", "span": "The object of worship of the Shrichakra is the realisation of the inseparateness (AbhedabhAvanA) of JnAtA which is HotA, of JnAna which is Arghya and of Jneya which is Havi^ JnAtd svAtmA bhavejjnAnam arghyang jneyang havii^ sthitam Shrichakrapujanang tejMm ekikara/zam iritam The scheme of SAdhanA and the different parts of the Shrichakra are explained in the VAsanA Pa/ala (Ch XXXV) of this work The T ripurAtApini Upani.s-^ad deals with KAyika and VAchika Karma and the BhAvanopani^/iad with Bhava", "atom_id": "pd.tantra_hist_pd.0f9688bf", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_6c509e6ef762", "family_id": "tantra_hist_pd", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for tantra_hist_pd (continue low family boost)", "span": "These\\ Siddhis are acquired by worship in the Chakra.\\ \\ Outside the sixteen pet^ils in the surrounding space of\\ the BhApura are ( see BhAvanopanijr/4ad S. 12 ) the ten\\ Mudrashaktis namely Sarvasang^yiobhiwi, SarvavidrAviwi,\\ SarvAkarj-Zya;/], SarvAveshakAri;/!, SarvonmAdini, MahAngkushA, Khechari, BijamudrA, MahAyoni, and 7 rikha//u^ikA, The first nine MudrAs belong to the nine Chakras of the Shri-Yantra respectively, the tenth being above all The nine MudrA Shaktis represent, that is are sha", "atom_id": "pd.tantra_hist_pd.7c82c328", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_97c7d18c7c81", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "After the account of the _first journey_ (xvii.-xix.) a short enumeration is made of the archangels, seven in number, and their functions (xx.) In the _second journey_ is described the place of final punishment of the fallen angels: \u201cThis place is the prison of the angels and here they will be imprisoned for ever.\u201d From thence Enoch is taken to Sheol; then to the west, where he sees the luminaries of heaven After that the angels show him \u201cseven magnificent mountains,\u201d upon one of which is the th", "atom_id": "pd.angel.ebfa6754", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_60fe58b0ad9e", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "There is no reason to begin with the book which happens to come first, especially as the first thirty-six chapters do not all belong together. ]( But, in any case, it will be found most useful to have some general idea of the contents of each of the different books before beginning to read them For this purpose a brief _r\u00e9sum\u00e9_ of each is given here. xxi. _The Book of Enoch_ (chapters xii.-xxxvi.) The book opens with a Dream-Vision of Enoch In this dream Enoch is asked to intercede for the watch", "atom_id": "pd.angel.489d2d71", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_49e2ae67e398", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "It is this, that the names were not chosen out of mere caprice; they indicated to a certain extent what subjects would be treated and the point of view of the writer.\u201d ]( Further, the fact that \u201cEnoch walked with God; and he was not; for God took him,\u201d ]( _i. e._ that he ascended into the heavens, is also significant; for he would thereby be just the one to know all about the heavenly luminaries; he was just the most appropriate author of a book which was to deal with astronomical questions. \u201cTh", "atom_id": "pd.angel.fc827aeb", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_df14040a09ee", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "Beer thinks that the \u201cDream-Visions\u201d (chapters lxxxiii.-xc.) belong to the time of John Hyrcanus (135-105 B.C.), and he includes under the pre-Maccab\u00e6an portions only xci. 12-17, xcii.xciii. 1-14; and holds that the rest of the book was written before 64 B.C Dalman maintains that it cannot be proved that the important section xxxvii.-lxxi. (the \u201cSimilitudes\u201d) is \u201cthe product of the pre-Christian period,\u201d though he fully xvrecognizes its Jewish character, Burkitt regards the writer as \u201calmost con", "atom_id": "pd.angel.9f88ac95", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_168f96d427a9", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "Despairing, therefore, of the world around them, the Apocalyptists centred their hope upon a world to come, where the righteous would come to their own and evil would find no place It is this thought which underlies the opening words of the Book of Enoch: \u201cThe words of the blessing of Enoch, wherewith he blessed the elect and righteous, who will be living in the day of tribulation, when all the wicked and godless are to be removed.\u201d Nowhere in this book is the essence of this hope more beautiful", "atom_id": "pd.angel.5cf47e23", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
{"pair_id": "pair_low_5560aa96acf1", "family_id": "angel", "role": "positive", "filler": "long primary PD excerpt for angel (continue low family boost)", "span": "It seems more likely that, with the exceptions already referred to, the various component parts of the book were written by Apocalyptists who belonged neither to Pharisaic nor yet to Sadduc\u00e6an circles. ### Language The Book of Enoch exists only in the Ethiopic Version; this was translated from the Greek Version, of which only a few portions are extant. ]( The Latin Version, which was also made from the Greek, is not extant, with the exception of i. 9, and cvi. 1-18; the fragment containing these", "atom_id": "pd.angel.128f7c3b", "epistemic": "OBSERVED"}
